<div class="bg-zinc-950 text-zinc-100" phx-no-curly-interpolation>
  <.marketing_nav current_user={@current_user} current={:docs} />

  <section class="py-12 sm:py-16">
    <.docs_layout
      current="connect-coop"
      updated="September 7, 2026"
      toc={[
        {"start", "Start the connection"},
        {"install", "Install co:op"},
        {"bridge", "Prepare the container"},
        {"configure", "Configure MCP"},
        {"verify", "Verify the connection"},
        {"troubleshooting", "Troubleshooting"}
      ]}
    >
      <.docs_header current="connect-coop">
        <:dek>
          co:op is a free, open-source tool for running AI agents locally in a sandbox.
          Pair it with emisar so your agent can work securely on your machine and with your infrastructure.
        </:dek>
      </.docs_header>

      <p class="mt-4 text-base leading-7 text-zinc-400">
        Without a sandbox, an agent can access files, secrets, and tools it doesn't need for
        its task. co:op gives it a separate workspace where you control what it can access.
        emisar extends that control to your infrastructure and third-party tools.
      </p>

      <.docs_prerequisites>
        <:item>
          A Linux or macOS computer with Docker running. This walkthrough uses a Docker
          image and named volume for the emisar bridge.
          The installer needs curl, tar, and sha256sum or shasum.
        </:item>
        <:item>An account with the coding-agent provider you want to use through co:op.</:item>
        <:item>
          An emisar account with permission to connect an agent and at least one runner online.
          The
          <.link navigate={~p"/docs/quickstart"} class="text-brand-400 hover:text-brand-300">quickstart</.link>
          sets up a runner and its first action.
        </:item>
      </.docs_prerequisites>

      <.docs_h2 id="start">1. Start the connection</.docs_h2>
      <p class="mt-4 text-base leading-7 text-zinc-400">
        Open
        <a
          href={~p"/app/agents/connect"}
          class="font-medium text-brand-300 underline decoration-brand-500/30 underline-offset-4 hover:text-brand-200"
        >Connect an agent</a>
        and choose <strong class="text-zinc-200">co:op</strong>.
        Your key is filled into the configuration automatically. Keep the page open while
        you prepare the container.
      </p>

      <.docs_h2 id="install">2. Install co:op</.docs_h2>
      <p class="mt-4 text-base leading-7 text-zinc-400">
        Run the installer in your host terminal. It downloads co:op into <.docs_inline_code>~/.local/bin</.docs_inline_code>, builds the container image,
        and runs
        <.docs_inline_code>coop doctor</.docs_inline_code>
        when a runtime is available.
        Follow
        <.link
          href="https://github.com/AndrewDryga/coop#install"
          class="text-brand-400 hover:text-brand-300"
        >co:op's installation guide</.link>
        for download verification and runtime setup.
      </p>
      <.docs_code
        phx-no-format
        label="shell"
        copy_text="curl -fsSL https://raw.githubusercontent.com/AndrewDryga/coop/main/install.sh | sh"
      ><span class="text-zinc-500">$</span> curl -fsSL https://raw.githubusercontent.com/AndrewDryga/coop/main/install.sh | sh</.docs_code>
      <p class="mt-4 text-base leading-7 text-zinc-400">
        Add
        <.docs_inline_code>~/.local/bin</.docs_inline_code>
        to your PATH if the installer
        asks. If it skipped the image build, run
        <.docs_inline_code>coop build</.docs_inline_code>
        followed by <.docs_inline_code>coop doctor</.docs_inline_code>.
      </p>
      <p class="mt-4 text-base leading-7 text-zinc-400">
        In the repository where you want to work, run
        <.docs_inline_code>coop init</.docs_inline_code>
        to create the project instructions, task queue, and hooks. Then run
        <.docs_inline_code>coop login codex</.docs_inline_code>
        and complete its sign-in prompts.
        Replace
        <.docs_inline_code>codex</.docs_inline_code>
        with <.docs_inline_code>claude</.docs_inline_code>, <.docs_inline_code>gemini</.docs_inline_code>,
        or
        <.docs_inline_code>grok</.docs_inline_code>
        for another provider.
      </p>

      <.docs_h2 id="bridge">3. Prepare the container</.docs_h2>
      <.docs_callout kind={:note} title="Keep production access behind emisar">
        <p>
          Keep production credentials, SSH-agent sockets, and the host's container socket out of
          the sandbox. Hide project-specific secret files with <.docs_inline_code>.coopignore</.docs_inline_code>;
          <.docs_inline_code>.gitignore</.docs_inline_code>
          does not hide them from the agent.
        </p>
      </.docs_callout>
      <p class="mt-4 text-base leading-7 text-zinc-400">
        The emisar bridge runs inside co:op's container and gives Codex, Claude, Gemini, and Grok
        the same MCP configuration. Build it into your image and mount a volume to retain
        rotated credentials when co:op replaces the container.
        Installing the bridge on your host does not install it in the image.
      </p>
      <.docs_h3 id="bridge-image">Build the bridge into your image</.docs_h3>
      <p class="mt-3 text-base leading-7 text-zinc-400">
        For a project without a custom image, create
        <.docs_inline_code>.agent/Dockerfile</.docs_inline_code>
        with this content. If you already have one, add the installation and directory-creation
        steps to it, keeping your existing toolchain and final user.
      </p>
      <.docs_code phx-no-format label=".agent/Dockerfile">ARG COOP_BASE_IMAGE=coop-box
FROM ${COOP_BASE_IMAGE}

USER root
RUN install -d -m 755 /etc/apt/keyrings \
 &amp;&amp; curl -fsSL https://cli.github.com/packages/githubcli-archive-keyring.gpg \
      -o /etc/apt/keyrings/githubcli-archive-keyring.gpg \
 &amp;&amp; chmod go+r /etc/apt/keyrings/githubcli-archive-keyring.gpg \
 &amp;&amp; printf '%s\n' "deb [arch=$(dpkg --print-architecture) signed-by=/etc/apt/keyrings/githubcli-archive-keyring.gpg] https://cli.github.com/packages stable main" \
      &gt; /etc/apt/sources.list.d/github-cli.list \
 &amp;&amp; apt-get update \
 &amp;&amp; apt-get install -y --no-install-recommends gh
RUN curl -fsSL https://emisar.dev/install-mcp.sh -o /tmp/install-mcp.sh \
 &amp;&amp; bash /tmp/install-mcp.sh --yes --install-dir /usr/local/bin \
 &amp;&amp; install -d -m 700 -o node -g node /config
USER node</.docs_code>
      <p class="mt-4 text-base leading-7 text-zinc-400">
        The installer verifies the release and
        <.docs_inline_code>--yes</.docs_inline_code>
        skips interactive sign-in during the image build. This Dockerfile installs GitHub CLI
        from its
        <.link
          href="https://github.com/cli/cli/blob/trunk/docs/install_linux.md#debian"
          class="text-brand-400 hover:text-brand-300"
        >official package repository</.link>
        so the installer can verify the release attestation. For a different base image, follow the
        <.link
          navigate={~p"/docs/connect-cli-agent#stdio-bridge"}
          class="text-brand-400 hover:text-brand-300"
        >bridge prerequisites</.link>
        and give your container user ownership of <.docs_inline_code>/config</.docs_inline_code>.
      </p>
      <.docs_h3 id="bridge-storage">Keep rotated credentials</.docs_h3>
      <p class="mt-3 text-base leading-7 text-zinc-400">
        In your host's <.docs_inline_code>~/.config/coop/coop.conf</.docs_inline_code>,
        select Docker and add the volume mount. If
        <.docs_inline_code>COOP_RUN_ARGS</.docs_inline_code>
        already exists, append the mount to its existing arguments. This setting applies to your
        co:op sessions on this machine.
      </p>
      <.docs_code phx-no-format label="coop.conf">COOP_RUNTIME=docker
COOP_RUN_ARGS=-v coop-emisar-config:/config</.docs_code>
      <p class="mt-4 text-base leading-7 text-zinc-400">
        Run
        <.docs_inline_code>coop build</.docs_inline_code>
        from your repository to build the
        extended image. Retain the
        <.docs_inline_code>coop-emisar-config</.docs_inline_code>
        volume: after rotation, the replacement key is stored there. If that volume is lost,
        create a fresh key in emisar and update the configuration before reconnecting.
        Run
        <.docs_inline_code>coop doctor</.docs_inline_code>
        again after changing the image or mounts
        to check the sandbox's isolation.
      </p>
      <.docs_h2 id="configure">4. Copy your co:op configuration</.docs_h2>
      <p class="mt-4 text-base leading-7 text-zinc-400">
        Back on <a
          href={~p"/app/agents/connect"}
          class="font-medium text-brand-300 underline decoration-brand-500/30 underline-offset-4 hover:text-brand-200"
        >Connect an agent</a>,
        copy the configuration under <strong class="text-zinc-200">Copy the MCP configuration</strong>.
        Your key is already filled in and is shown only during setup.
      </p>
      <p class="mt-4 text-base leading-7 text-zinc-400">
        co:op reads
        <.docs_inline_code>~/.config/coop/agents/mcp.json</.docs_inline_code>
        on your host and supplies those servers to the agent inside its container.
        Merge the emisar entry into that file, preserving any other servers.
        Create the file and parent directories if they are missing, and make the file readable
        and writable only by your user. Keep it outside your repository;
        <.docs_inline_code>COOP_MCP_FILE</.docs_inline_code>
        can select another private file. The console generates this shape with literal values:
      </p>
      <.docs_code phx-no-format label="mcp.json">{
  "mcpServers": {
    "emisar": {
      "command": "/usr/local/bin/emisar-mcp",
      "env": {
        "EMISAR_URL": "https://emisar.dev",
        "EMISAR_API_KEY": "emk-replace-with-your-key",
        "EMISAR_CLIENT": "coop",
        "XDG_CONFIG_HOME": "/config"
      }
    }
  }
}</.docs_code>
      <p class="mt-4 text-base leading-7 text-zinc-400">
        For signed dispatch, add
        <.docs_inline_code>EMISAR_SIGNING_KEY</.docs_inline_code>
        and
        <.docs_inline_code>EMISAR_SIGNING_CERT</.docs_inline_code>
        to that
        <.docs_inline_code>env</.docs_inline_code>
        object using the values from <.link
          navigate={~p"/docs/signed-dispatch"}
          class="text-brand-400 hover:text-brand-300"
        >Set up signed dispatch</.link>.
        Keep credentials in this private host configuration, outside your Dockerfile and repository.
      </p>

      <.docs_h2 id="verify">5. Verify the connection</.docs_h2>
      <ol class="mt-4 list-decimal space-y-3 pl-6 text-base leading-7 text-zinc-400">
        <li>
          From your repository, start a fresh session with <.docs_inline_code>coop codex</.docs_inline_code>, <.docs_inline_code>coop claude</.docs_inline_code>, <.docs_inline_code>coop gemini</.docs_inline_code>, or <.docs_inline_code>coop grok</.docs_inline_code>.
          Check that emisar appears in the agent's MCP tools.
        </li>
        <li>
          Ask the agent: <strong class="font-medium text-zinc-200">Use emisar to find a runner
          with linux.uptime, run that action, and show me the output.</strong>
          Allow the tool call if your agent asks, and complete any approval required by your policy.
        </li>
        <li>
          Confirm the returned uptime, then open
          <a
            href={~p"/app/audit"}
            class="font-medium text-brand-300 underline decoration-brand-500/30 underline-offset-4 hover:text-brand-200"
          >Audit</a>
          and check the action, runner, and operator. Listing MCP tools checks discovery;
          the action output and audit event confirm execution.
        </li>
      </ol>

      <.docs_h3 id="tool-permissions">Skip local tool-call prompts</.docs_h3>
      <p class="mt-3 text-base leading-7 text-zinc-400">
        co:op's default commands for Codex, Claude, Gemini, and Grok already skip local
        permission prompts for all tools inside the sandbox, including emisar.
        This does not change your <.link
          navigate={~p"/docs/policies-and-approvals"}
          class="text-brand-400 hover:text-brand-300"
        >emisar policies and approvals</.link>.
      </p>
      <p class="mt-4 text-base leading-7 text-zinc-400">
        If you've customized the launch command and want to return to co:op's default behavior:
      </p>
      <ol class="mt-4 list-decimal space-y-3 pl-6 text-base leading-7 text-zinc-400">
        <li>
          Check your shell environment and co:op configuration file for your agent's command setting: <.docs_inline_code>COOP_CODEX_CMD</.docs_inline_code>, <.docs_inline_code>COOP_CLAUDE_CMD</.docs_inline_code>, <.docs_inline_code>COOP_GEMINI_CMD</.docs_inline_code>, or <.docs_inline_code>COOP_GROK_CMD</.docs_inline_code>.
          The file is
          <.docs_inline_code>~/.config/coop/coop.conf</.docs_inline_code>
          by default,
          or
          <.docs_inline_code>$XDG_CONFIG_HOME/coop/coop.conf</.docs_inline_code>
          when set.
          <.docs_inline_code>COOP_CONF</.docs_inline_code>
          can select another file.
          An environment variable takes precedence over the file.
        </li>
        <li>
          Remove only that command override from the file and unset it in your shell, if present.
          This restores the whole default command, not just its permission setting.
        </li>
        <li>
          Start a new session with <.docs_inline_code>coop codex</.docs_inline_code>, replacing
          <.docs_inline_code>codex</.docs_inline_code>
          with your agent.
        </li>
      </ol>

      <.docs_h2 id="troubleshooting">Troubleshooting</.docs_h2>
      <ul class="mt-4 list-disc space-y-3 pl-6 text-base leading-7 text-zinc-400">
        <li>
          <strong class="text-zinc-200">No container runtime:</strong>
          install and start Docker, then run
          <.docs_inline_code>coop build</.docs_inline_code>
          and <.docs_inline_code>coop doctor</.docs_inline_code>.
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong class="text-zinc-200">401 after connecting:</strong>
          check the key and the credential volume mount. Replace a revoked or expired key in
          the private configuration, then start a new co:op session.
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong class="text-zinc-200">Bridge executable not found:</strong>
          rebuild the project's co:op image and start a new container. A host executable path is
          not a container executable path.
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong class="text-zinc-200">Tools are listed but there are no actions:</strong>
          check that a runner is online and that your access and pack trust allow the action.
          Follow
          <.link navigate={~p"/docs/troubleshooting"} class="text-brand-400 hover:text-brand-300">Troubleshooting runs</.link>
          for policy and approval failures.
        </li>
      </ul>
    </.docs_layout>
  </section>

  <.marketing_footer />
</div>
